@database "skills"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: skills.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Skills"
@{h1}Skills

A skill is a folder of agent knowledge: a @{tt}SKILL.md@{utt} with the
instructions, and often further files next to it -- references, scripts,
templates -- that are only worth reading when a task needs them. This
plugin gives an agent two tools to browse that knowledge: one lists the
skills, or the files one skill carries, the other reads a skill's
instructions or one of its files. It only reads; it changes nothing and
stores nothing. It has no hooks and no panel.

What skills are, where they are found, and how an agent gets one into its
prompt (@{tt}always@{utt}, @{tt}on_demand@{utt}) or a person runs one from
the chat (@{tt}/skill-name@{utt}) is part of the core, see
@{" Skills " link scarabhive/skills}. An agent whose skills all sit in its
prompt as @{tt}always@{utt} does not need this plugin; an agent with
@{tt}on_demand@{utt} skills does, because those arrive only as a one-line
entry and the agent loads the rest with these tools.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Listing skills  " link list}
  @{"  Reading a skill  " link read}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} is there, switched on:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    skills:
      type: skills
      enabled: true
@{body}

Allow the tools in an agent's list:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+skills/*"
@{body}

@{h2}Which skills it sees

The plugin sees every skill the core finds, not only the ones the agent
names in its @{tt}skills@{utt} block: an agent with these tools can list
and read any of them. The roots are @{tt}skills.skill_dirs@{utt} in
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}, the same list the prompt uses. When that key
is missing or empty, the environment variable
@{tt}AGENT_SKILL_DIRS@{utt} (roots separated by the system's path
separator) is used, and without that, @{tt}skills@{utt} and
@{tt}.claude/skills@{utt}. When two roots hold a skill of the same name,
the first root wins.

The roots are scanned once per process, and again only when the list of
roots changes. A skill added later, or a changed name, description or
version in a @{tt}SKILL.md@{utt} header, shows up after a restart. The
files themselves are read at every call: an edited instruction or
reference is seen at once, and so is a file added to a skill already known.

@{h2}Server settings and dependencies

None. The plugin has no settings of its own -- the read limit of 100,000
characters per call is fixed in the code -- and needs nothing beyond the
core.
@endnode

@node list "Listing skills"
@{h1}Listing skills

@{tt}skills_list@{utt} (the prefix follows the plugin instance) answers
which skills there are, and what files one of them carries. Without a name
it lists all of them, each with name and description only; with a name,
that one in detail, files included.

@{h2}Parameters

The framework does not check arguments against the schema; this is what
the code does with them.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
name | a skill's name; spaces around it are removed. Missing, null or empty: all skills. A value that is not text is taken as text (5 is "5")
@{body}

@{h2}The answer

Without a name: @{tt}status@{utt} "success", @{tt}count@{utt} and
@{tt}skills@{utt}, sorted by name, each entry only @{tt}name@{utt} and
@{tt}description@{utt}. With a name: @{tt}status@{utt} and
@{tt}skill@{utt}, one entry with all of these fields:

@{table}
Field | Meaning
name | the name from the @{tt}SKILL.md@{utt} header; the folder's name when the header has none or one that breaks the naming rules
version | the header's @{tt}version@{utt} or @{tt}metadata.version@{utt}; "0.0.0" without either
description | the header's description, at most 1,024 characters; without one, the first paragraph of the instructions
tags | the header's @{tt}tags@{utt} (a list or comma-separated text); empty without
files | the files in the skill's folder and its subfolders, as paths relative to the folder with @{tt}/@{utt}, sorted; @{tt}SKILL.md@{utt} included
@{body}

The file list leaves out hidden files and folders (a name starting with a
dot), @{tt}__pycache__@{utt} folders and a leftover @{tt}skill.toml@{utt}
manifest. Everything else is listed, also
files that cannot be read as text, such as images.

An unknown name answers @{tt}status@{utt} "error" with
@{tt}error@{utt} "Unknown skill '<name>'", plus " — did you mean
'<other>'?" when one name is close enough, @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt} (that
name or null) and @{tt}available@{utt} (all names).

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"name": "tdd"}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "success",
 "skill": {"name": "tdd", "version": "0.0.0",
           "description": "Test-driven development. Use when ...",
           "tags": [],
           "files": ["SKILL.md", "agents/openai.yaml", "mocking.md", "tests.md"]}}
@{body}
@endnode

@node read "Reading a skill"
@{h1}Reading a skill

@{tt}skills_read@{utt} reads a skill's instructions, or one file it
carries.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
name | required; the skill's name, spaces around it removed. A value that is not text is taken as text
path | a file in the skill, relative to its folder (@{tt}references/catalog.md@{utt}); spaces around it removed. Missing, null or empty: the instructions from @{tt}SKILL.md@{utt}
offset | where to start, in characters of the text described below; 0 when missing, null or empty. A whole number >= 0, also as text ("5"); anything else is an error. Equal to the text's length: empty content; beyond it: an error
@{body}

Without @{tt}path@{utt} the answer is the instructions without the YAML
header and without the blank lines after it -- the agent already has name
and description from the list. With @{tt}path@{utt} "SKILL.md" it is the
whole file, header included. Any file is read as UTF-8; a byte order mark
at the start is dropped.

@{h2}What may be read

Only files inside the skill's folder. A path is refused when it is
absolute, or when it leads out of the folder -- with @{tt}..@{utt} or
through a link that points elsewhere; both sides are resolved first. A
folder is not a file and answers "not found".

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | "success" or "error"
skill | the skill's name
path | the path asked for, or "SKILL.md"
truncated | true when text is left after this piece
content | at most 100,000 characters, starting at @{tt}offset@{utt}
next_offset | only when @{tt}truncated@{utt}: the offset to send next (@{tt}offset@{utt} + 100,000)
@{body}

A longer file is read in pieces: call again with the same @{tt}name@{utt}
and @{tt}path@{utt} and @{tt}offset@{utt} set to @{tt}next_offset@{utt},
until an answer has no @{tt}next_offset@{utt}. The pieces joined are the
whole text. Offsets count characters of the text as handed over -- for
the instructions without @{tt}path@{utt}, that is the text without its
header.

@{h2}Errors

All errors are answers with @{tt}status@{utt} "error" and
@{tt}error@{utt}:

@{table}
error | When, and what else comes with it
'name' is required | no name, or only spaces; @{tt}available@{utt}
Unknown skill '<name>' | as with the list: @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt}, @{tt}available@{utt}
'<path>' must be relative to the skill directory | an absolute path; @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt}, @{tt}files@{utt}
'<path>' escapes skill '<name>' | the path leads out of the folder; @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt}, @{tt}files@{utt}
'<path>' not found in skill '<name>' | no such file; @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt}, @{tt}files@{utt}
Cannot read '<path>': <reason> | the file is not UTF-8 text, or reading it failed; nothing else
'offset' must be a whole number >= 0, got <value> | anything but plain digits (at most 18): a negative number, one with a decimal point (5.0 too), other text; nothing else
'offset' <n> is past the end (<length> chars) | the offset lies beyond the text; nothing else
@{body}

For the three path errors, " — did you mean '<file>'?" is added when a
file of the skill is close enough -- @{tt}reference/@{utt} where the skill
has @{tt}references/@{utt}, for instance. @{tt}files@{utt} is the skill's
file list, as in @{" Listing skills " link list}.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"name": "tdd", "path": "mocking.md"}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "success", "skill": "tdd", "path": "mocking.md",
 "truncated": false, "content": "# Mocking\n\n..."}
@{body}
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Tool and parameter descriptions come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; together they are about 1,130 characters in every
request of an agent that has the tools.

An agent with @{tt}on_demand@{utt} skills also gets an entry in its
system prompt, written by the core: "Available skills", a line per skill
with its description, and the sentence that it can read them with
@{tt}<instance>_read@{utt} and list their files with
@{tt}<instance>_list@{utt} -- the names of the tools this agent has.
An agent with @{tt}on_demand@{utt} skills but without these tools gets no
entry at all; the log shows an error, once per agent, to allow them.

@{h2}The status line

@{table}
Call | The chat shows
list, all | "3 skill(s) available: a, b, c", or "no skills found (searched <roots>)"
list, one | "tdd v0.0.0: 4 file(s) — SKILL.md, agents/openai.yaml, mocking.md, tests.md"
read | "Reading tdd/mocking.md", then "tdd/mocking.md — 2345 of 2345 chars", with " from <offset>" when an offset was given and " (truncated, next_offset <n>)" when text is left
error | the error text; path and offset errors begin with the skill's name, "tdd: ..."
@{body}

The end lines are cut to 140 characters, ending in " …": a long list of
skills or files would not fit one row.

@{h2}Size of an answer

The list of all skills carries name and description only; with the 42
skills of this repository that is about 14,500 characters, most of it
descriptions (up to 1,024 characters each). It carried every file of every
skill before -- 273 files and about 25,700 characters -- and the files now
come with a name: 4,200 characters for the largest skill (61 files). A
read is at most 100,000 characters of content.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are ordinary tool results, appended to the history; nothing
earlier is changed, so the prompt cache stays intact.

@{h2}Skills are instructions

What a skill says, the model is meant to follow. A skill copied in from
elsewhere is trusted the moment its folder lies under a root: this plugin
hands its text over unchanged. A skill's header may name
@{tt}allowed-tools@{utt}; this system does not restrict the agent's tools
by it (the core logs a warning).
@endnode
